WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.100
When a laser emits beams of light particles
called photons at a screen with two slits,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.760
you’d expect to see two strips to correspond
with the two slits.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.940
Instead, we see a pattern of light and dark
bands.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.570
This suggests that light behaves like waves
that pass through both slits simultaneously,

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.570
interfering with each other to create the
pattern on the second screen.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:25.170
However, when scientists used a measuring
device to observe the slit that each photon

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:30.330
passed through, the interference pattern disappeared,
and two strips appeared.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:36.300
The act of observing which slit it went through
changed the behavior of the photons!

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.770
No one knows why observing a particle causes
its behavior to change.

